# Source for the Front's heterogeneous coalition and narrow common program:
# Fakhreddin Azimi, Iran: The Crisis of Democracy, 1941–1953, ch. 5,
# pp. 250–267. The recurring meeting and its effects are counterfactual.
title: Common Resolution
new-page: true
is-card: true
tags: party_affairs
view-if: common_resolution_timer <= 0 and front_formed = 1
on-arrival: month_actions += 1; common_resolution_timer = 4

= Draft a Common Resolution

The {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}National Front{!</span>!}
must speak collectively without pretending that its component organizations
share one complete program. A resolution can hold to constitutional ground,
concentrate on oil, or publish the limits of agreement alongside the common
demand.

The wording will determine which allies can defend the statement in their own
organizations and which work the coalition postpones.

- @resolution_constitution
- @resolution_oil
- @resolution_amendments

@resolution_constitution
title: Restate the constitutional minimum
subtitle: The coalition binds itself to a rule all four components can defend, while oil waits
on-arrival: constitutional_legitimacy += 4; iran_party_relation += 2; independent_nationalists_relation += 2

The resolution confines itself to representative elections, political
liberties, and government through constitutional institutions. Each component
can support that minimum without abandoning its wider program.

The common ground becomes more credible, but the statement gives no new
urgency to the oil campaign.

- @post_event: Continue

@resolution_oil
title: Make national control of oil the common demand
subtitle: A single national demand creates momentum—and tests allies who joined for more than oil
on-arrival: oil_coalition_support += 5; public_mandate += 3; toilers_dissent += 1; iran_party_dissent += 1

The resolution places national control of oil at the center of the
{!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front's{!</span>!} immediate
work. Newspapers and deputies receive a direct demand they can repeat.

Some allies object that one issue is beginning to stand in for the coalition's
constitutional and social commitments. Oil support rises while the internal
argument sharpens.

- @post_event: Continue

@resolution_amendments
title: Publish the common text with component amendments
subtitle: Each organization keeps its own voice beside the common text, at the cost of clarity
on-arrival: iran_party_dissent -= 2; toilers_dissent -= 2; religious_network_dissent -= 2; press_capacity += 2

The {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} publishes a
short common resolution followed by signed statements from organizations that
want to explain their own reasons or reservations. Disagreement is visible
without being mistaken for a collapse of cooperation.

The format eases internal pressure and gives friendly papers material for
different audiences. It lacks the force of one unqualified declaration.

- @post_event: Continue
